llama.cpp (as fetched by Ollama v0.35.1, llama.cpp b11232): a 4-byte integer
dot product for gfx803's decode kernels built from SDWA byte selects.

WHY
---
ggml_cuda_dp4a is the inner operation of every quantized dot product. gfx906
and later have v_dot4_i32_i8; gfx900 and RDNA1 get an inline-assembly
sequence that ends in v_add3_u32. gfx803 has neither, so it takes the generic
C++ path, which unpacks both operands into int8x4_t and multiplies byte by
byte. In the decode kernels keeping the unpacked bytes live costs registers:
in the stock build 56 of the gfx803 mmvq kernels use more than 128 VGPRs (one
wave per SIMD) and two spill, mostly the 2-8 column variants that speculative
and parallel decoding use (q2_K at 4 columns: 198 VGPRs; at 7 columns: 256
with 10 spilled), and the q8_0 flash-attention vec kernels spill too (D=128,
2 columns: 256 VGPRs, 46 spilled).

gfx803's VOP2 instructions take SDWA operand selects with sign extension, so
v_mul_i32_i24 can multiply byte i of one register by byte i of another
directly: four multiplies and four adds give the exact signed dot product
with no unpacked copies kept live. K-quant kernels also call
dp4a(0x01010101, x, c) to sum the four signed bytes of x; for a compile-time
0x01010101 operand two SDWA adds of byte pairs and one or two plain adds do
that without multiplies. mmvq kernels above 128 VGPRs drop from 56 to 1,
spilling ones from 2 to 0, and the flash-attention kernel above to 193 VGPRs
with no spills.

Everywhere else the compiler's byte-wise code is faster, so it stays there:
the mmq prompt kernels (with SDWA, q8_0 at 512 columns took 26.3 instead of
21.7 ms, the IQ types 12% longer, q6_K 5%) and the dot products whose operand
comes from get_int_from_table_16 (iq4_nl, iq4_xs, mxfp4, nvfp4; iq4_xs
decode was 1.3-2.6x slower with SDWA). Why SDWA loses there is not known.

test-backend-ops perf on an RX 590, MUL_MAT 4096x14336, us per run for
1/2/4/8/512 columns, without -> with this patch and 105:
  q4_K              188/257/445/877/24564 -> 187/261/447/827/24577
  q5_K              233/287/474/892/29395 -> 224/278/469/849/29471
  q6_K             269/341/559/1079/27511 -> 268/355/572/957/27426
  q8_0              283/288/426/591/21608 -> 284/303/347/623/21552
  q2_K             212/335/731/2137/35882 -> 218/376/669/1324/35869
  q3_K             257/342/809/1131/29738 -> 247/356/569/1009/29578
  iq4_xs            152/189/403/809/24475 -> 152/188/402/810/24385
Decode at 4-8 columns gains up to 1.6x where the old kernels needed more than
128 VGPRs; one column, plain generation, is unchanged; 2 columns lose 1-11%;
prompt processing (512 columns) and iq4_xs are unchanged.

Checked against the CPU backend with test-backend-ops: MUL_MAT 1301/1301,
MUL_MAT_ID 931/931, FLASH_ATTN_EXT 3982/3982.

WHAT
----
In ggml/src/ggml-cuda/common.cuh, a gfx803 branch in ggml_cuda_dp4a: when
either operand is the constant 0x01010101, a sign-extended SDWA byte sum of
the other operand plus c; otherwise four v_mul_i32_i24 with byte selects
accumulated with v_add_u32 (both clobber vcc, which the asm declares).
ggml_cuda_dp4a_plain is the generic byte-wise product on gfx803 and
ggml_cuda_dp4a elsewhere; ggml_cuda_dp4a_sel<plain> picks one of the two at
compile time.

In vecdotq.cuh the mmq-only helpers (the *_impl_mmq K-quant functions,
vec_dot_q8_1_q8_1_impl, vec_dot_q8_0_16_q8_1_impl) and the iq4_nl, iq4_xs,
mxfp4 and nvfp4 dot products call ggml_cuda_dp4a_plain; the q4_0, q4_1 and
q8_0 helpers shared by mmq and mmvq take a plain template flag, which
mmq-vec-dot.cuh sets. Other architectures compile exactly what they did
before.

